/// The most specific overload wins, whatever order they were declared in.
///
/// Resolution today is first-declared-wins.  With Take(Any) written above
/// Take(Integer), the call Take(1) selects the Any one; swap the declarations
/// and the same call selects the Integer one.  Both processors agree, so this
/// is a shared design rather than a divergence.
///
/// ⚠️ Declaration order deciding which function runs is a hazard on its own,
/// but it also fights the compiler: a static resolver naturally picks the most
/// specific candidate, so leaving the run time order-dependent guarantees the
/// two paths disagree the moment direct calls are emitted.
///
/// The rule is exact match, then widening, then Any.  This file writes Any
/// FIRST on purpose -- under the intended rule that placement changes nothing.
///
/// Fails with: Assertion 'left = right' failed.  Expected 'exact' but got 'any'.
///
class Chooser;
begin
    function Take (A : Any)     : String; begin Exit 'any';   end
    function Take (N : Integer) : String; begin Exit 'exact'; end
end

test 'The most specific overload wins regardless of order';
begin
    AssertEqual ('exact', Chooser ().Take (1));
    AssertEqual ('any',   Chooser ().Take (Str ('x')));
end
